diff --git a/kombatant/rtx/RTX-TESTING.md b/kombatant/rtx/RTX-TESTING.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/kombatant/rtx/RTX-TESTING.md
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Experimental DLSS Super Resolution + hardware ray-traced AO
+
+Windows x64, NVIDIA RTX GPU and a recent NVIDIA driver are required. This is a test build, not a performance release. Both features start OFF every time. No driver installation or system changes are performed.
+
+1. Extract the complete portable build into a writable folder. Do not mix files from other builds.
+2. Start at a 1920x1080 window, manual render resolution set to window, with motion blur, regular AO, depth of field and shader-overlay effects disabled while testing.
+3. Preferences > Display > Experimental RTX: select DLSS Quality. The status must say **DLSS running**. If the driver/runtime rejects the request, it turns off and logs the reason.
+4. Test moving, turning, resizing the window, teleporting, and switching Quality/Balanced/Performance. Look for smearing, jitter, upside-down images, ghosting around avatars or a black screen.
+5. Turn on Hardware ray-traced ambient occlusion separately. The status must say **Hardware DXR AO running**. Compare a corner between two nearby solid world objects with the option off/on.
+6. Send the viewer log, your NVIDIA driver version, resolution, selected options and screenshots when reporting a problem. Do not publish logs without removing account/session details.
+
+## What is implemented
+
+- The separate `KombatantRTX64.dll` calls NVIDIA NGX DLSS Super Resolution through DirectX 12. It supplies color, depth, per-pixel motion, camera jitter, history-reset requests and full-window output before UI rendering. Quality renders at about 67%, Balanced 58%, Performance 50% per axis. Manual supersampling is overridden while DLSS is requested. The release NVIDIA `nvngx_dlss.dll` is pinned and signature/hash checked by the build.
+- Hardware AO builds DXR bottom/top level acceleration structures from nearby, non-rigged opaque volume triangles. A DXR 1.1 inline ray-query compute shader traces four hemisphere rays per pixel and shades the result.
+
+## Known limits
+
+- OpenGL/D3D12 frame transfers currently synchronize and copy through CPU memory. Expect substantially lower FPS, especially above 1080p. This prototype proves the integration path; zero-copy shared resources are not implemented.
+- DXR scene coverage is limited to non-rigged world volumes within 64m, capped at 200,000 triangles and refreshed twice per second. Avatars, attachments, flexible geometry, terrain, alpha blended/masked surfaces are excluded. Moving objects can have a half-second occlusion lag. Glass/material alpha can still be approximated as opaque in some pools. Four rays are noisy; there is no standalone AO denoiser.
+- This is **DLSS Super Resolution**, not Frame Generation or Ray Reconstruction. Ray-traced reflections and global illumination are not implemented.
+- DLSS currently runs on display-referred color after existing world post-processing. Existing velocity passes do not cover every animated surface; camera reprojection fills missing vectors, which can ghost on those surfaces. A reactive/transparency mask and motion-vector dilation are not implemented.
+- Output is capped at 4096 pixels per axis. Do not combine this test with 8K/12K supersampling.
+- RTX GPU allocations stay alive until viewer exit. Disabled controls stop evaluations; restarting clears the module's GPU allocations and retries a failed device.
+
+If startup or display fails, close the viewer and run `Start-Without-RTX.cmd`. To bypass the shader overlay too, use `Start-Without-FX.cmd` or set `KOMBATANT_DISABLE_FX=1` in the same launcher.
+
+The viewer remains LGPL. The independent RTX bridge is MIT-licensed; NVIDIA's SDK/runtime retain the separate NVIDIA license under `licenses/NVIDIA-DLSS-LICENSE.txt`.
